  "lineage": "Brazilian Northeast rural-pastoral rhythm-and-song-form codified 1946-onward by accordion-zabumba-triangle pé-de-serra trio-instrumentation, named after the rural-stick-dance baião already documented 19th-century in Pernambuco-and-Bahia agreste-interior. The characteristic baião zabumba-pattern emphasizes the third sixteenth (the 'lifted' beat) and the eighth-note dotted accentuation, with sanfona-accordion delivering descant melody-and-chord-accompaniment. Lyrically focused on rural-Northeast everyday-life: drought (seca), cattle-ranching (vaqueiro), migration to Southern Brazilian cities, religious-folkloric culture (cordel literature, feast-days). The 1960s-MPB-tropicalia generation reframed baião as a national-symbol of Brazilian-northeastern identity in opposition to coastal-cosmopolitan bossa-nova-and-Rio-elite.",
